 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "SNAB.2 — Examining a work of art: Looks at Grandma's paintings one by one, connects the subject of the work with their own life (the flowers in the pot, their own family), and expresses feelings and thoughts about the work and explains why (SNAB.2.g: “It made me cheerful, because it's warm!”).",
   "SNAB.3 — Valuing works of art and artists: Makes predictions about how the works were made and checks them (the flowers and the family were painted from life, the house from imagination); visits the exhibition at home, looks at the works with their eyes and appreciates the effort of their painter grandma.",
   "OB9 Art Literacy — Recognises concepts such as exhibition, painter, visitor and palette; “reads” what a painting tells.",
   "SDB2.3 Social Awareness — Notices from her grandma's red cheeks and sudden quietness that she feels shy, and acts accordingly (holds her hand, looks slowly and carefully, listens to her).",
   "D14 Respect — Shows respect for someone else's effort and work: looks at the works without touching, listens to the artist, gives their opinion politely."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Show a painting, photo or postcard from home or the classroom: “What do you see in this picture? How does it make you feel?”",
   "On the cover page, ask: “What is an exhibition? How do we behave when we visit one?”"
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, count the paintings together: one, two, three. “Why might Grandma have opened an exhibition?”",
   "Stop on page 2: “Grandma's cheeks went red and she rubbed her fingers. How might she be feeling?” Name the child's answer: “Yes, a little shy, a little nervous.”",
   "Accept both answers to the prediction on page 3; find out together on page 4: the flowers and the family were painted from life, the house from imagination.",
   "On page 5, before touching each painting, ask the child: “How did this painting make you feel? Why?” Then listen to Zeynep's answer and compare."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Which paintings were in Grandma's exhibition?",
   "Why did Grandma go quiet? How did Zeynep treat her?",
   "Which painting did you like most? Why?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Class Exhibition: The Real Thing Behind My Picture",
   "sure": "25 min",
   "materyal": "Drawing paper, pastel crayons, tape or pegs, string; a few real objects from the classroom (a potted plant, a toy, fruit)",
   "adim": [
    "Put a few real objects on the table; each child draws a picture by looking at one of them or by imagining.",
    "Hang the pictures on the wall or on a string and turn the classroom into a gallery. The children take turns being “painter” and “visitor”; the visitors walk round slowly with their hands behind their backs.",
    "The visitors stop in front of a picture and complete the sentence “This picture made me feel …, because …”; the painter then tells what they looked at or imagined to make it."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Who was Grandma's first visitor?",
   "How did Zeynep know her grandma felt shy?",
   "How do you feel when you look at a painting?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "A little exhibition at home",
   "d": "Hang your child's or your family's pictures on a wall, on the fridge or on a string with pegs. Make your child the first visitor; stop in front of each picture and ask “How does this picture make you feel, and why?” Then swap roles: they are the painter, you are the visitor."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Grandma's Art Show”. Zeynep's painter grandma hung her paintings in the living room and opened an exhibition at home. Zeynep noticed that her grandma felt shy; she looked at the paintings slowly, found out which were painted from life and which from imagination, and said how each painting made her feel, and why. At home, you too can open a little exhibition, look at pictures with your child and share your feelings."
 },
